;;; `std.algebra`'s `connex` over a relation of the program's own that the
;;; blind enumerator proposes: a program that is not monotone.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `std.algebra` imported for `connex`, over `lt`, a relation the program
;;; declares over three nodes and states two facts of, `(lt A B)` and `(lt B
;;; C)`. It names no obligation and activates none, and nothing closes `lt`;
;;; the import loads `std.algebra`'s two obligation rules with the module.
;;; A and C are not compared, so `connex` concludes `(false)` at root.
;;;
;;; ── What is said of it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; No hypothesis rule, and no obligation activated at root, so the blind enumerator
;;; builds H, and it proposes `lt`: `(lt B A)`, `(lt C A)`, `(lt C B)` and
;;; `(lt A C)`. An obligation rule a program loads and nothing activates
;;; chooses no rung. `connex` concludes `(false)` from an `absent` over
;;; `lt`, a relation a commitment can still extend, so the static check
;;; flags the program: it loads, every command answers it with the warning
;;; that it is not monotone, and nothing is claimed of its answer
;;; (Q-M1f.564). `(solve :no-hypothesis (lt))` would close `lt`, and the
;;; program would be monotone.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(import std.algebra :symbols (connex))
(relation lt Node Node :why "{?3} is less than {?4}")
(instance A Node) (instance B Node) (instance C Node)
(connex lt instance Node)
(lt A B) (lt B C)
(query :goal (lt ?x ?y))
